# Configuration for @marketpulse/frontend.
#
#   cp apps/frontend/.env.example apps/frontend/.env
#
# The destination matters. Vite loads `.env` from `envDir`, which
# `vite.config.ts` states explicitly as `"."` — so it is `apps/frontend/.env`,
# beside this file, symmetric with `apps/backend/.env`. A `.env` at the
# repository root is read by neither package, silently.
#
# There is nothing to set yet. The application reads no configuration at all:
# it makes no request and knows no host. Story 1.12 gives it a backend to call
# and brings the first entry here.
#
# `import.meta.env.BASE_URL` is not one of these. It is one of Vite's own
# built-ins, set from `base` in `vite.config.ts`, and no line in this file
# affects it.

# --- Only VITE_-prefixed names reach the browser ---
#
# `envPrefix: ["VITE_"]` in `vite.config.ts`. Everything else in this file is
# invisible to client code, and "invisible" is stronger than it sounds: the
# read is substituted away at build time, not merely left undefined. Measured
# in Task 1.6.6 against the built artefact, with a realistic secret planted
# here and both values referenced from `src/main.tsx`:
#
#   console.log(`probe`, void 0, `https://api.marketpulse.example/v1`)
#
# The prefixed value is a string literal in `dist/assets/*.js`. The
# non-prefixed one is `void 0`, and neither the secret nor even the name
# `ALPACA` appears anywhere in the artefact. The Storybook build behaves
# identically, which matters because `pnpm build` produces it too and
# `storybook-static/` serves from a static host just as `dist/` does.
#
# Two things defeat this and neither is configured. Widening `envPrefix`, and
# `define`, which substitutes whatever it is given with no prefix rule at all.
# `pnpm env:check` fails the build if a non-prefixed name appears in this file,
# so the whitelist has a check behind it rather than a convention.

# --- Credentials do not belong in this file. Any of them. ---
#
# Market-data and model-provider credentials are server-side without exception.
# The browser talks to the MarketPulse backend; it never talks to Alpaca or to
# a model provider. Epic 2's Alpaca keys and Epic 10's LLM key go in
# `apps/backend/.env` and stay there.
#
# A `VITE_` prefix does not make a secret safe. It makes it a string literal in
# a file every visitor downloads. The prefix is a boundary against accidents,
# not a permission; adding one to a credential name is how the accident becomes
# a decision.
